          %h2#semantics Semantics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Construct a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Tarski+universes"} weak Tarski universes
            %li
              %p
                Construct an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} (infinity,1)-topos
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} elementary (infinity,1)-topos
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1507.02648"} 1507.02648
                has shown that we obtain a locally cartesean closed quasicategory.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                proposed the following question: “Given an (infinity-)functor from (any flavor of) homotopy type theories to infinity-categories as above, characterize its essential image.”
            %li
              %p
                Define a notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} elementary (infinity,1)-topos
                and show that any such admits a model of homotopy type theory.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
                for strict
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resizing+rules"} resizing rules
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/~vladimir/Site3/Univalent_Foundations_files/2011_Bergen.pdf"} Bergen lecture
            %li
              %p
                Construct (higher)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive-recursive+type"} inductive-recursive types
                in univalent models, or show that they don’t exist.
            %li
              %p
                Connect
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametricity"} parametricity
                as suggested
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://bentnib.org/dtt-parametricity.pdf"} here
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.05027"} here
            %li
              %p
                Show that when the construction of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
                in chapter 10 of the book is interpreted in the
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    simplicial set model
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/simplicial+set+model"} ?
                %a{:href => "http://math.andrej.com/2014/01/13/univalent-foundations-subsume-classical-mathematics/comment-page-1/#comment-34342"} this comment
                and surrounding discussion.
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+invariance+problem"} model invariance problem
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                chosen to present an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
          %h2#metatheory Metatheory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                “Writing all inductive definitions in terms of Sigma-types and W-types is theoretically possible, but extremely tedious … it’s unknown whether any similar reduction for HITs is possible.” (
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2014/06/08/hiru-tdd/"}> source
                )
            %li
              %p
                Give a
                %span.newWikiWord
                  computational interpretation
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/computational+interpretation"} ?
                of univalence and HITs. The cubical set model makes progress on this.
                %a{:href => "http://www.cse.chalmers.se/~coquand/mod1.pdf"} PDF
            %li
              %p
                Is there a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
                that describes more exactly what happens in the
                = succeed "?" do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    model of type theory in cubical sets
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/model+of+type+theory+in+cubical+sets"} ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/simhu/cubical"} cubical language
                %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/lb14cubical/lb14cubes-oxford.pdf"} this
                and
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~txa/publ/ctt.pdf"} this
            %li
              %p
                Show in HoTT that the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi n
                        %mi th
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
                universe is a model of HoTT with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
                universes. Part of this question is: what is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2014/03/03/hott-should-eat-itself/"} internal model of HoTT
            %li
              %p
                What is the
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theoretic+strength+of+univalent+type+theory+plus+HITs"} proof theoretic strength of univalent type theory plus HITs
                %a{:href => "http://reports-archive.adm.cs.cmu.edu/anon/2014/CMU-CS-14-101R.pdf"} here
                before continuing to, say, pushouts, the cumulative sets, the Cauchy reals and the surreals.
            %li
              %p
                Is univalence consistent with Induction-Recusion? This would allow us to build a non-univalent universe inside a univalent one. Related to this, higher inductive types
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2014/06/08/hiru-tdd/"} can
                be used to define a univalent universe.
            %li
              %p
                Consider the type theory with a sequence of universes plus for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\gt 0
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-type"} n-types
                that are not
                = succeed "-types;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1311.4002"} here
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
                increase the logical strength? Of course, univalence gives funext.
            %li
              %p
                Prove or disprove the conjecture that every fibrant type in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HTS"} HTS
                is equivalent to one definable in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} MLTT
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+invariance+problem"} model invariance problem
                positively for MLTT but negatively for fibrant types in HTS.
            %li
              %p
                Define
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theory"} higher observational type theory
          %h2#homotopy_theory_and_algebraic_topology Homotopy theory and algebraic topology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Homotopy theory in HoTT
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Homotopy+theory+in+HoTT"} ?
            %li
              %p Similarly to the torus, consider the projective plane, Klein bottle, … as discussed in the book (sec 6.6). Show that the Klein bottle is not orientable. (This requires defining “orientable”.)
              %ul
                %li This also requires defining what a surface is.
            %li
              %p
                Calculate more
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups of spheres
            %li
              %p
                Show that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups of spheres
                are all finitely generated, and are finite with the same exceptions as classically.
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Reid+William+Barton"} Barton
                and
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Tim+Campion"} Campion
                have proved the first part of this, and the result has been
