          %h2#closed_problems Closed problems
          .query
            %p How about keeping a running list of solutions like this:?
            %p Maybe only things not in the HoTT book? Else the list of solved problems gets very long!
            %p OK, here’s the rule: if it was stated here (or on the UF-wiki) as an open problem, then it gets recorded here once it’s solved.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+semi-simplicial+sets"} model structure on semi-simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                Prove that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
                (with the HIT definition involving a 2-dimensional path) is equivalent to a product of two circles. See Sojakova’s proof:
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/torus.pdf", :title => "torus.pdf"} torus.pdf
                %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2015/01/20/ts1s1-cubically/"} here
            %li
              %p Construct the Hopf fibration. (Lumsdaine gave the construction in 2012 and Brunerie proved it was correct in 2013.)
            %li
              %p
                Construct the “super
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+Hopf+fibration"} quaternionic Hopf fibration
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 3
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 7
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3 \to S^7 \to S^4
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01134"} homotopy version of the Cayley-Dickson construction
            %li
              %p
                Prove the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seifert-van+Kampen+theorem"} Seifert-van Kampen theorem
            %li
              %p
                Construct Eilenberg–MacLane spaces and use them to define cohomology. (Licata and Finster did it in 2013, written up in
                %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/lf14em/lf14em.pdf"} this paper
                (pdf).)
            %li
              %p
                Define the Whitehead product. Guillaume Brunerie did this in 2017, written up in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10307"} this paper
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_4( S^3)= Z/n Z
                for some
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                and a construction of the
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/EHP+spectral+sequence#via_the_james_model"} reduced James product
                as a HIT and a homotopy colimit. It is also proven that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega \Sigma X \simeq J(X)
                for some pointed connected type
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.05916"} his thesis
            %li
              %p
                Does having an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
                with only typal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+conversion"} beta conversion
                rules imply
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
                in
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
                only have a typal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+conversion"} eta conversion
                rule? Answered in the negative in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/interval+type#relation_to_function_extensionality"} this section about the relation between the interval type and function extensionality
            %li
              %p
                Is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+domain"} boolean domain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
                rules, the
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~mhe/truncation-and-extensionality/hsetfunext.html"} here
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/interval+type#relation_to_propositional_truncations"} this section about the relation between the interval type and propositional truncations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝟚
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝕀
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:\mathbb{2} \to \mathbb{I}
                and use double induction on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝟚
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{2}
                to inductively define a function
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo :
                            %mn 2
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi b
                            %mo :
                            %mn 2
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo =
                          %mi 𝕀
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\prod_{a:2} \prod_{b:2} j(a) =_\mathbb{I} j(b)
            %li
              %p
                Define the Hurewicz map and prove the Hurewicz theorem. A proof of the Hurewicz theorem in HoTT can be
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.05833"} found here
            %li
              %p
                What is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+of+a+wedge+of+circles"} loop space of a wedge of circles
                indexed by a set without decidable equality? It is a set, and can be shown to be using the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12339"} zigzag construction
            %li
              %p
                Treat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+types"} coinductive types
                in HoTT. Part of the problem is that general co-inductive types are not fully understood in ordinary type theory. Using extensionality, we obtain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-types"} M-types
                from W-types. Resolved by Ahrens, Capriotti &amp; Spadotti by defining coinductive types using
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+inductive+types"} indexed inductive types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+schema"} axiom schema
                using the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+quantifier"} uniqueness quantifier
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
                such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+Agda"} cubical Agda
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narya"} Narya
